{"causes":["Around 2008–2010, the global publishing industry underwent significant changes with the rise of e-books and digital distribution platforms, which ultimately paved the way for novels like A Dance with Dragons to reach a wider audience.","The events of the preceding books in the A Song of Ice and Fire series created an insatiable demand for more content from George R. R. Martin, culminating in his decision to publish A Dance with Dragons.","The growing popularity of fantasy fiction as a genre, particularly among younger readers, contributed to the novel's success by catering to this expanding market."],"effects":["The publication of A Dance with Dragons marked a significant milestone in the proliferation of e-books and digital publishing, demonstrating the potential for online platforms to democratize access to literature.","A Dance with Dragons played a substantial role in shaping the modern fantasy genre, influencing numerous authors and contributing to its growing popularity among readers worldwide.","The novel's global impact has been felt through the creation of numerous fan conventions, cosplay events, and other cultural phenomena that celebrate the series and its characters.","A Dance with Dragons has also had a lasting impact on popular culture, inspiring countless pieces of fan art, fan fiction, and music inspired by the series."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"george-r-r-martin","sourceName":"George R. R. Martin","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"a-dance-with-dragons","targetName":"A Dance with Dragons","context":"Martin's most recent novel in the A Song of Ice and Fire series."},{"sourceSlug":"amazon","sourceName":"Amazon","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"george-r-r-martin-a-dance-with-dragons-2011","targetName":"George R. R. Martin: A Dance with Dragons (2011)","context":"The online retail giant played a pivotal role in the novel's release and subsequent success."},{"sourceSlug":"fantasy-faction.com","sourceName":"Fantasy Faction","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-dance-with-dragons-by-george-r-r-martin","targetName":"A Dance with Dragons by George R. R. Martin","context":"The online community site provided an in-depth analysis of the novel's plot, characters, and themes."},{"sourceSlug":"game-of-thrones","sourceName":"Game of Thrones","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-dance-with-dragons","targetName":"A Dance with Dragons","context":"The HBO television adaptation of the novel played a significant role in its popularity and commercial success."},{"sourceSlug":"fantasy-faction.com","sourceName":"Fantasy Faction","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-dance-with-dragons-by-george-r-r-martin","targetName":"A Dance with Dragons by George R. R. Martin","context":"The online community site highly recommended the novel for its engaging storyline and well-developed characters."},{"sourceSlug":"goodreads.com","sourceName":"Goodreads","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-dance-with-dragons-by-george-r-r-martin","targetName":"A Dance with Dragons by George R. R. Martin","context":"The popular book review platform featured the novel on its list of best-selling and highly-rated books."},{"sourceSlug":"game-of-thrones.com","sourceName":"Game of Thrones","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-dance-with-dragons","targetName":"A Dance with Dragons","context":"The television adaptation of the novel served as a major promotional vehicle for the book, generating significant interest and buzz among fans worldwide."},{"sourceSlug":"fantasy-faction.com","sourceName":"Fantasy Faction","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"a-dance-with-dragons-by-george-r-r-martin","targetName":"A Dance with Dragons by George R. R. Martin","context":"The online community site featured a series of articles and interviews discussing the novel's themes, characters, and plot twists."}],"places":[{"name":"New York City","role":"Major publication event"},{"name":"London","role":"Distribution hub for print copies"},{"name":"Belfast","role":"Martin's hometown"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: A Dance with Dragons","type":"Reference article","slug":"a-dance-with-dragons"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q28306","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Dance_with_Dragons"],"tags":["literary work","Novels & Prose Fiction","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"A Dance with Dragons contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"A Dance with Dragons is the fifth novel in George R. R. Martin's A Song of Ice and Fire epic fantasy series, published on 12 July 2011 by Bantam Books — after a six-year gap following A Feast for Crows (2005) and the longest publication interval in the series to that point. A Dance with Dragons runs concurrently with A Feast for Crows (covering the same timeline as the fourth book), then extends beyond it, following the characters absent from the fourth volume: Jon Snow's increasingly difficult command at Castle Black and the Wall, Daenerys Targaryen's troubled rule of Meereen and her struggle to control her dragons, Tyrion Lannister's exile journey eastward, Bran Stark's training beyond the Wall with the Three-Eyed Crow (Brynden Rivers), and Stannis Baratheon's northern campaign. The novel also introduces Dorne's plot (the 'Queenmaker' scheme) and picks up several storylines from A Feast for Crows after the timeline catches up.

A Dance with Dragons is notable for its structural complexity — its parallel-then-sequential relationship with A Feast for Crows created a demanding narrative architecture that Martin has described as the most difficult structural problem he faced in the series. The novel's most significant narrative events include Jon Snow's controversial decisions at the Wall (admitting the wildlings, alliance with the Free Folk) that culminate in his assassination by his own Night's Watch brothers; Daenerys's departure from Meereen astride the dragon Drogon; Tyrion's passage through the ruins of Valyria and his encounter with the stone men (greyscale); and the revelation of Jon Snow's true parentage (strongly implied through the 'R + L = J' foreshadowing). The 'Pink Letter' — Ramsay Bolton's letter to Jon claiming victory at Winterfell — precipitates Jon's decisive action and his subsequent assassination.

A Dance with Dragons sold over 170,000 copies in its first day of publication and debuted at #1 on the New York Times bestseller list — the commercial peak of A Song of Ice and Fire before the HBO series (Game of Thrones, 2011–2019) generated even larger readership. As of 2025, the sixth volume (The Winds of Winter) remains unpublished, making A Dance with Dragons the last completed novel in the series.","subjects":["Fantasy Fiction","Modern Era","George R. R. Martin","Epic Fantasy","21st Century","American Literature","Television Adaptation","Series Fiction"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"A Dance with Dragons represents a pivotal moment in the evolution of modern fantasy literature, as it capitalized on emerging trends in digital publishing and global connectivity.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
